cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  degradepredict: calibrated limit-crossing forecasts for a degrading
  subsystem, with right censoring handled and measured
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-16
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Forecasting of the time at which a degrading housekeeping health metric
  crosses a declared limit, with the prediction interval around that date
  measured rather than asserted. Three analytic baselines are implemented and
  validated first: a straight-line fade, an exponential fade, and a
  physics-shaped sublinear power-law capacity fade with the exponent taken from
  Wang et al. (2011). All three reduce to ordinary least squares of a
  transformed metric on a transformed time, so the crossing time is an inverse
  regression and is reported with both the delta-method interval and the exact
  Fieller (1954) confidence set, including the unbounded case that arises on
  17.59 per cent of randomly drawn windows. On correctly specified data the
  Fieller interval attains between 0.8835 and 0.9405 coverage against a nominal
  0.90 over eight configurations, and the delta-method interval under-covers at
  0.8565 in precisely the configuration where the exact set is often unbounded.
  A gradient-boosted quantile forecaster on nineteen lag and shape features
  produces a distribution over the crossing time and is benchmarked against all
  three baselines on held-out seeded trajectories. The contribution that makes
  the benchmark honest is censoring. Most units have not crossed their limit
  yet, scoring only the ones that have is survivorship bias, and this package
  therefore handles right censoring explicitly through inverse probability of
  censoring weighting and reports coverage, CRPS and pinball loss separately
  for complete and right-censored trajectories. The result is that the
  comparison has two different winners. On the 173 complete test trajectories
  the learned forecaster wins, with a CRPS of 30.80 days against 90.74 for the
  best baseline. On the 227 right-censored trajectories, 56.75 per cent of the
  test set, the exponential baseline wins, with a CRPS of 346.37 days against
  435.56, on all three independent seed pairs measured. That baseline win is
  reported rather than removed. Interval coverage at a nominal 0.90 is 0.7630
  on complete trajectories and 0.3348 on censored ones for the learned model,
  so the pooled figure of 0.5200 describes neither half; no model in the
  repository is probabilistically calibrated, the best Kolmogorov-Smirnov
  distance of the probability integral transform from uniformity being 0.1970
  on complete trajectories against a sampling floor of 0.0398. Holding the
  cited power-law fade exponent fixed at 0.55 when the truth is 0.45 biases the
  crossing estimate by 181 days on a 1200-day crossing while the reported
  interval is 62 days wide and covers the truth 0.0000 of the time, which is
  the clearest statement available of what a declared physical form costs when
  it is slightly wrong. Finally, only 0.0396 of the learned model's censored
  intervals are refuted by the censoring alone, against a true non-coverage of
  0.6652, so a deployment measuring what it can measure would see about six per
  cent of its own failure. Validation level 2, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - degradation-modelling
  - remaining-useful-life
  - limit-crossing
  - right-censoring
  - prediction-intervals
  - quantile-regression
  - spacecraft-telemetry
  - autonomous-aerospace-systems
